---
# Certificates for the PUBLIC auth hostnames, issued into the Traefik namespace (IN-16 follow-up).
#
# WHY TRAEFIK NEEDS A CERT FOR A HOSTNAME CLOUDFLARE ALREADY TERMINATES:
# a browser reaching auth-dev.fitmate.me terminates TLS at Cloudflare, so Traefik has never needed
# one. But the website's Auth.js BFF refreshes tokens SERVER-SIDE, from inside the cluster, using
# the issuer URL — which is https://auth-dev.fitmate.me. Sending that pod to Cloudflare means it
# meets the Access login page instead of a token. Sending it to Traefik instead (split-horizon DNS)
# only works if Traefik can present a certificate for that name which the pod trusts; otherwise the
# request fails with "unable to get local issuer certificate" and we have traded one failure for
# another. Verified 2026-08-23 — Traefik's websecure entrypoint has TLS on, but only a self-signed
# default cert.
#
# A publicly-trusted certificate is used deliberately rather than an internal CA: pods trust it out
# of the box from the base image's CA bundle, so no application change, no CA injection, and no
# insecureSkipVerify anywhere.
#
# Rendered only for hosts the caller passes, so this is inert until an environment opts in.
%{ for host in try(parameters.public_host_certs, []) ~}
apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
kind: Certificate
metadata:
  # Secret and Certificate share a name derived from the hostname, so the mapping is obvious in
  # `kubectl get certificate -n traefik` without cross-referencing anything.
  name: ${replace(host.name, ".", "-")}-tls
  namespace: ${namespace}
spec:
  secretName: ${replace(host.name, ".", "-")}-tls
  issuerRef:
    name: ${try(host.issuer, "letsencrypt-dns01")}
    kind: ClusterIssuer
  dnsNames:
    - ${host.name}
---
%{ endfor ~}
